WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.089 --> 00:00:09.920
Of all the polarizing issues in politics todays,
and of course, there are many, the climate

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:11.920
debate is at the top of the list.

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:16.679
Climate change seems to be one of the 'you're
either with us, or you're against us' topics,

00:00:16.679 --> 00:00:20.550
usually debated by people immediately taking
a partisan stance rather than a well thought

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:21.869
out position.

00:00:21.869 --> 00:00:25.690
This past year, I lost a good friend who told
me that she could no longer be associated

00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:30.480
with people who will even debate climate change,
and I'm someone who has had such conversations

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:31.869
on this very show.

00:00:31.869 --> 00:00:37.960
I think the most hate I ever got was when
I had Alex Epstein, author of The Moral Case

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:40.620
for Fossil fuels in for a sit down.

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:45.910
Alex's argument isn't against man made climate
change, instead he's more focused on how to

00:00:45.910 --> 00:00:49.170
increase human prosperity with what we have
right now.

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:53.400
Essentially, his argument is although climate
change could be real, fossil fuels still have

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:55.850
many net benefits for our society.

00:00:55.850 --> 00:01:01.440
After endless tweets, emails and messages
from angry people, Alex did a follow up video

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:05.350
himself where he answered around a 100 viewer
questions.

00:01:05.350 --> 00:01:10.719
Engaging with people who have different views
is what the battle of ideas is all about.

00:01:10.719 --> 00:01:14.630
On the flip side of the debate, I've also
sat down with Michael Mann, a climatologist

00:01:14.630 --> 00:01:19.439
and the author of the famous 'The Hockey Stick
and the Climate Wars', who is a thought leader

00:01:19.439 --> 00:01:21.229
in the climate change debate.

00:01:21.229 --> 00:01:25.740
I've discussed climate change to some degree
with believers in man made climate change

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:30.280
like Sam Harris, Michael Shermer, Richard
Dawkins as well as some with a more skeptical

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:34.399
view such as Andrew Klavan, Stephen Crowder
and Ben Shapiro.

00:01:34.399 --> 00:01:38.669
And for the record not one of these guests
believe the exact same thing as anyone else

00:01:38.669 --> 00:01:40.249
I just mentioned.

00:01:40.249 --> 00:01:45.020
I find climate change to be a really interesting
discussion because it often shows how ones

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:47.759
thinking works in other areas as well.

00:01:47.759 --> 00:01:52.090
Generally speaking, Democrats or those on
the Left see climate change as man made and

00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:57.579
believe governmental action is the best, or
in some cases the only way, to reverse its

00:01:57.579 --> 00:01:58.869
negative effects.

00:01:58.869 --> 00:02:03.209
Republicans, or those on the right, generally
believe that something is happening to the

00:02:03.209 --> 00:02:07.469
environment, though there is some debate as
to whether it is man made, and in either case,

00:02:07.469 --> 00:02:11.130
believe that the private sector is better
left to deal with these issues.

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:15.590
Of course there are outliers on each side,
such as my former guest, an California Democratic

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:20.590
gubernatorial candidate Mike Shellenberger
who wants the private sector to be more involved

00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:22.879
in solving climate change issues.

00:02:22.879 --> 00:02:26.629
And of course, there are also Republicans
and people on the right who deny man made

00:02:26.629 --> 00:02:28.739
climate change at all.

00:02:28.739 --> 00:02:33.250
The science debate on climate change is an
interesting one, and one in which I'm happy

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:34.250
to have.

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:39.030
What interests me more though, is how government
should, or should not, be part of the solution

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:41.230
to whatever the problem may be.

00:02:41.230 --> 00:02:44.989
You know my feelings on this, that the government
is rarely the solution to anything.

00:02:44.989 --> 00:02:48.760
Actually, at this point, I think the government
is usually the problem in the first place,

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:53.740
and I'd always rather exhaust private options
before turning to the government, especially

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:55.239
the federal government.

00:02:55.239 --> 00:02:59.569
Ironically, this should be the position of
the people who hate Trump, why would you want

00:02:59.569 --> 00:03:03.630
to keep giving the government more power when
you hate the guy in charge, but at the end

00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:08.060
of the day its not governmental power they
have a problem with, its that they only have

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:12.939
a problem with governmental power when its
not their guy in charge.

00:03:12.939 --> 00:03:17.810
Fortunately, there seems to be a younger generation
that is tackling issues like climate change

00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:20.530
and the environment in a totally new way.

00:03:20.530 --> 00:03:24.959
It seems to be based not only in scientific
facts but also in an understanding of how

00:03:24.959 --> 00:03:26.999
a limited government should work.

00:03:26.999 --> 00:03:32.430
My guest this week is Benji Backer, President
of the American Conservation Coalition.

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:35.969
The group is dedicated to finding new ways
to look at old problems.

00:03:35.969 --> 00:03:42.189
As its mantra states, they are committed to
efficient, effective, conservative environmentalism.

00:03:42.189 --> 00:03:46.150
As for me, I am a believer in the science
behind climate change, and the funny thing

00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:49.499
about science is that it doesn't care whether
you believe in it or not.

00:03:49.499 --> 00:03:53.470
For me the, real debate around climate change
is how to go about finding solutions without

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:57.159
giving more power and money to government.

00:03:57.159 --> 00:04:01.939
Whether you believe in climate science or
not, whether you want more government regulation

00:04:01.939 --> 00:04:07.139
or less, or whether you believe the private
section can find solution or not, we're all

00:04:07.139 --> 00:04:09.880
still stuck on this rock together.

00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:14.810
So until Elon Musk, a private businessman
gets us all to Mars, let's try to figure out

00:04:14.810 --> 00:04:19.521
how to solve some of our problems on this
pale blue dot while we still have the chance...

